  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Given the curve $r(t)=\u27e8{t}^{2}+2t,4-{t}^{2}\u27e9,$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $\u27e80,-1\u27e9.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4-{t}^{2})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82t+2,-2t\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\u27e80,-1\u27e9$ and ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $\u27e80,-1\u27e9\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e80,-1\u27e9\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t) & =0 \\\\ \u27e80,-1\u27e9\\cdot \u27e82t+2,-2t\u27e9 & =0 \\\\ 0(2t+2)+(-1)(-2t) & =0 \\\\ 2t & =0 \\\\ t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=0.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\u27e8{0}^{2}+2(0),4-{0}^{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e80,4\u27e9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\u27e80,-1\u27e9$ and ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(0,4).$",
